# 109 - Incremental Rebuild Detection

**Phase:** 1 (Foundation)
**Effect:** Builds only happen when needed, saving 30+ minutes
**Status:** Completed (2025-02-24)

---

## The Effect

The update command checks for remote changes before rebuilding. If nothing changed upstream, it skips the 30-minute rebuild.

Use `--force` to rebuild anyway (first compile, local changes, troubleshooting).

---

## What This Solved

### Before

Two modes:
- `install` - Clone fresh, always rebuild (60 minutes)
- `update` - Pull latest, always rebuild (30 minutes)

**Problems:**
1. `update` rebuilds even when nothing changed
2. No way to build without pulling (first compile after clone)
3. Wastes time: "No remote changes → 30 minute rebuild anyway"
4. Developer frustration: "Why am I waiting?"

Workflow looked like:
```bash
$ azerothcore update
Fetching updates...
Already up to date.
Rebuilding anyway... (30 minutes)
# Why?!
```

### After

Smart update with `--force`:
```bash
$ azerothcore update
Fetching updates...
No remote changes found. Skipping rebuild.

$ azerothcore update --force
Fetching updates...
No remote changes found.
Forcing rebuild... (30 minutes)
```

---

## Implementation

### Remote Change Detection

```bash
# Fetch latest from origin
git fetch origin

# Compare local HEAD to remote HEAD
LOCAL=$(git rev-parse HEAD)
REMOTE=$(git rev-parse origin/main)

if [[ "$LOCAL" != "$REMOTE" ]]; then
    echo "Remote changes detected. Pulling and rebuilding..."
    git pull
    make -j${MAKE_JOBS}
elif [[ "$FORCE" == "true" ]]; then
    echo "No changes, but forcing rebuild..."
    make -j${MAKE_JOBS}
else
    echo "No changes. Skipping rebuild."
fi
```

### Behavior Table

| Scenario | Without --force | With --force |
|----------|-----------------|--------------|
| Remote has changes | Pull + rebuild | Pull + rebuild |
| No remote changes | Skip rebuild | Rebuild anyway |
| First build (binaries missing) | Skip rebuild | Rebuild |

---

## The --force Flag

### When to Use

**First compile after clone:**
```bash
git clone https://github.com/.../wow-chat-2
cd wow-chat-2
azerothcore update --force  # No binaries exist yet
```

**After local code changes:**
```bash
# Edited src/lua/ambush.lua
azerothcore update --force  # Rebuild with changes
```

**Troubleshooting broken build:**
```bash
# Something went wrong, binaries corrupted
azerothcore update --force  # Clean rebuild
```

**Testing without pulling:**
```bash
# Want to rebuild current version
azerothcore update --force  # Don't pull, just build
```

### When NOT to Use

**Daily development:**
```bash
# Check for updates
azerothcore update  # Only rebuilds if upstream changed
```

Most of the time, you DON'T want `--force`. The script is smart enough.

